# const say: (...values: unknown[]) => void = (globalThis as any).print ?? console.log;
# say(said.length + " lines " + digest(said.join("\n")));
# -- end of source
# `inc` is copied in line into the body of a for-of that may go by protocol, so the copy stands under the cleanup that closes the iterator and throws on. That cleanup reads nothing of the turn: its edge brings no argument.
# Where a guess of the copy fails and nothing was done since the copy began, the copy leaves for the call, as it does where no handler stands; the call stands under the cleanup like the call that was written.
# RUN: walk f1 mechanisms=all
# CHECK: frequency=Rare isInLoop=true handler=
# CHECK: = CallDirect {{*}}function=f6 [isNeverInlined]
# What the call throws closes an iterator once and a list never; a getter in the place of `at` runs as often as in the source.
# RUN(ANSWERS): run-in-shell
# ANSWERS: 60 lines 2050998429
